---
/**
 * FEATURE QUOTE — slice R7. THE PIVOT.
 *
 * CONTEXT.md Section Composition table, row "Feature Quote":
 *
 *   Entrance      the pivot itself
 *   Column window 2-10
 *   Image         NONE — type on Obsidian
 *   Rhythm        LARGEST ON THE PAGE
 *   Margin holds  the folio goes quiet
 *   Length        pull quote only
 *
 * THIS IS NOT A CONTENT SECTION AND IS NOT BUILT LIKE ONE. Everything
 * above it is her own work; everything below it is the funnel index.
 * The entrance IS the pivot: no kicker, no subtitle, no rule, no
 * numeral, no decorative mark, no button, no photograph. One element,
 * one edge — the second of CONTEXT.md's two sanctioned exceptions to
 * the three-left-edges staircase.
 *
 * THE FAILURE MODE IS "THIN DARK STRIPE", AND IT IS A SCALE-AND-SPACE
 * PROBLEM, NOT A STYLING ONE. The band's height is 150px of padding
 * top and bottom plus the type, and the type is the largest on the
 * page outside the Cover's own name. Nothing else is needed and
 * nothing else is added — see the texture note in the stylesheet.
 *
 * COPY. Verbatim from COPY.md, "Site-wide → Pull quote (Feature
 * Quote)". One line, nothing else in the section. No attribution, no
 * source, no name, no dash, no second sentence.
 *
 * QUOTATION MARKS ARE NOT SET, AND THIS IS A SETTING DECISION RATHER
 * THAN A REWORDING — the string is untouched. COPY.md's marks are the
 * deck's notation for "this is the quote", the same way its other
 * entries are blockquoted. Two reasons they do not ship:
 *
 *   1. THE SECTION IS THE QUOTE. A full-bleed Obsidian band holding
 *      one italic line at display scale, with the page's own work
 *      above it and the index below, is already unambiguous. Marks at
 *      112px are redundant punctuation at the loudest point on the
 *      page.
 *   2. THEY BREAK THE LEFT EDGE. The one thing this section's
 *      composition rests on is a single hard left edge inset one
 *      column from the page trim. An opening quote mark either sits
 *      inside that edge — in which case the whole block reads as
 *      indented — or hangs outside it on a negative text-indent, in
 *      which case the section's only edge is carried by a punctuation
 *      mark. The old build did the second (`text-indent: -0.42em`, a
 *      measured advance for one face at one tier) and it is a
 *      swept constant of exactly the kind CONTEXT.md's standing rules
 *      keep deleting: it goes stale the moment the size, the axis
 *      settings or the face change.
 *
 * FLAGGED FOR JACKSON'S CONFIRMATION in the R7 report.
 *
 * THE <h2> IS THE PULL QUOTE. CONTEXT.md's locked heading structure
 * makes this explicit: "the chapter break's <h2> carries the pull
 * quote, so a screen-reader user jumping by heading lands on the
 * page's thesis at the structural pivot." Not a <blockquote> wrapper
 * with a hidden heading beside it, and not a <p>. The <blockquote> is
 * kept for semantics — it is a quotation — and the <h2> sits inside
 * it carrying the text, so the two agree instead of duplicating.
 *
 * No `cite` attribute and no <footer>: there is no attributed source.
 * The line is her own, on her own page, and COPY.md forbids adding an
 * attribution line.
 *
 * GOLD — 3 of 3. "Legacy" sets in Antique Gold, the rest of the line
 * in Porcelain. CONTEXT.md's pinned budget names the pull quote as
 * one of the three saturated gold type accents at rest, so this is
 * the budgeted use and not an addition; the count is unchanged from
 * the spec. It rhymes with the Cover's "Where Leadership Meets
 * Legacy", where the same word is the page's first gold. The whole
 * line is NOT gold — that would spend the accent as a fill, which the
 * palette forbids in as many words — and no other gold enters this
 * section: no rule, no mark, no hairline.
 *
 * NO PHOTOGRAPH. This section previously carried athletics-detail.jpg
 * as a full bleed. That image is Athletic Management's `inset` now
 * and is referenced in exactly one place in the codebase.
 *
 * NO HEIGHT FLOOR. R1.4 is a standing rule: the Cover is the only
 * section on this page with a height constraint. The band is padding
 * plus type and that is the whole of it.
 *
 * NO MOTION. No IntersectionObserver, no opacity: 0 initial state, no
 * transition of any kind. There is nothing interactive in the section
 * to have a state.
 *
 * NO HAND-SET grid-column ANYWHERE IN THIS FILE. The single placement
 * is `.win-quote` in global.css, whose behaviour in all four bands is
 * written out above the implementation there.
 */
---

<section class="sheet quote" id="sec-quote">
  <blockquote class="win-quote quote-block">
    <h2 class="quote-line">Every decision is viewed through a larger lens: <em
        class="legacy">Legacy</em>.</h2>
  </blockquote>
</section>

<style>
  /* ---- THE GROUND ------------------------------------------------
     Full-bleed Obsidian, edge to edge of the viewport. `.quote` is a
     top-level section and no ancestor of a section carries a
     max-width, a centring margin-inline or a container wrapper, so
     the background is the viewport width by construction — there is
     no 100vw and no `margin-left: calc(50% - 50vw)` here, both of
     which measure the viewport INCLUDING the scrollbar and would ship
     horizontal overflow. Porcelain page ground above and below.

     FLAT #1A1613. NO TEXTURE, and this is the constraint that has to
     survive the next pass as much as this one.

     The old build's PullQuote.astro layered two radial gradients over
     this ground — an 88%x72% gold wash at 78% 10% and a 70%x60% slate
     wash at 6% 94%. Both are gone and neither is to come back. Three
     rules converge on it:

       - CONTEXT.md, Design Direction: "Drama comes from type scale
         and negative space, never from colour volume." A wash IS
         colour volume, and it is spent on the one section that has
         nothing but scale and space to work with.
       - CONTEXT.md, R1.1 standing rule: "If a treatment must be
         uniform across a frame, use a flat colour, not a ramp" — and
         the measured failure behind it, that a gradient's corner
         renders as its first stop whatever the stop positions. The
         gold wash above was anchored at the top-right corner of a
         full-viewport band; at 1920 that corner is the brightest
         thing in the section and it is not on the type.
       - The gold budget. A 0.13-opacity gold radial over a
         full-viewport band is not a type accent and is not a
         hairline, so it is a use the pinned budget has no bucket for.

     The frontend-design skill says never default to a solid
     background and to reach for gradient meshes, noise and grain.
     CONTEXT.md overrides it here and CONTEXT.md wins — its own words,
     against its own measurement. The page's grain sheet in
     BaseLayout.astro is a fixed sibling of the whole document and
     already crosses this band; it is the page's texture, not this
     section's, and nothing is added on top of it.

     NO gradient. NO mesh. NO noise or grain overlay of its own. NO
     vignette. NO radial wash. NO border. NO rule. Do not improve it.

     ---- THE VALUE STEP -------------------------------------------
     Both boundaries are hard and stay hard. CONTEXT.md's 35-50
     luminance-step window governs where a PHOTOGRAPH meets the page
     ground — it exists because a contained rectangle of studio
     seamless reads as pasted on. This is a designed colour band, not
     a frame, and its ~200-unit step against Porcelain is the entire
     mechanism of the page turn. Nothing here is softened, feathered
     or transitioned.

     ---- PADDING ---------------------------------------------------
     150px top and bottom, the largest on the page, per CONTEXT.md's
     per-section table: Cover 64 · About 96 · Educational Leader 110 ·
     Premier 80 · Everyday Legends 70 · Athletic 110 · FEATURE QUOTE
     150 · Index 110 · Blog 110/40 · Contact 96. Sections MUST NOT
     share a padding token, so the value is set here and nowhere else.

     NO min-height, NO svh, NO viewport-height floor of any kind.
     ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
  .quote{
    background-color: #1A1613;
    padding-block: 150px;
  }

  /* The <blockquote> is a semantic wrapper only. Every browser gives
     it a 40px inline margin by default, which would put the type on a
     fourth left edge one arbitrary distance in from its own column —
     the exact failure CONTEXT.md's three-edges rule is written
     against. Zeroed, so the grid area IS the text block. */
  .quote-block{
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* ---- THE TYPE --------------------------------------------------
     Fraunces, ITALIC, quote tier. EVERY AXIS IS ENUMERATED, in the
     rule and in the font request in BaseLayout.astro, for both the
     roman and the italic face: `--fx-quote` is
     `'opsz' 96, 'wght' 340, 'SOFT' 40, 'WONK' 1`, which is
     CONTEXT.md's optical table for this role exactly. Fraunces
     defaults to wght 900, WONK on and SOFT 100 when an axis is left
     unstated, and a Google Fonts URL requesting only ital,opsz,wght
     ships full roundness silently — so nothing here is left to the
     face to decide.

     WONK 1 is the point of the tier, and it is spent here and nowhere
     else. In italic it swaps in the flagged ball terminals on b d h k
     l v and w — this line has all of b, d, h, l and w in it. The
     Cover's own gold "Legacy" is deliberately set at WONK 0 for
     exactly this reason: the flourish belongs to the pivot.

     `font-weight: 340` restates the wght axis in the property the
     browser uses for fallback selection, so a Montserrat-less,
     Fraunces-less render is not 900-weight. It agrees with the axis
     rather than overriding it.

     ---- SCALE -----------------------------------------------------
     THE LARGEST TYPE ON THE PAGE AFTER THE COVER'S OWN NAME, and by a
     long way over everything it is actually measured against. The
     page's ledes all resolve from `--lede-size`,
     `clamp(52px, 3.8vw, 64px)` — 54.72px at 1440 — and this is
     112.3px there, 2.05x the largest lede on the page and 6.6x the
     17px body. CONTEXT.md's lede-to-body floor is 3x; this clears it
     against the LEDES.

     7.8vw between a 44px floor and a 128px ceiling:
       1920  128.0  (ceiling; 7.8vw would be 149.8)
       1640  127.9  (the ceiling engages here)
       1440  112.3
       1280   99.8
       1024   79.9
        751   58.6
        390   44.0  (floor; 7.8vw would be 30.4)

     The ceiling exists because the line is a fixed number of
     characters: past about 1640 the type stops needing to grow to
     dominate and starts running out of column instead. The floor
     exists because the phone measure is 340px at 390 and a smaller
     size buys nothing — it is already the largest type on the page
     there too.

     `line-height: 1.02` — display type sets solid. `text-wrap:
     balance` so the rag is even rather than leaving "Legacy." alone
     on a line; the final word is the gold one and an orphan of it
     would read as a caption. */
  .quote-line{
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-variation-settings: var(--fx-quote);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 340;
    /* --quote-size, promoted to a token in global.css by R14/R15.
       THE VALUE IS UNCHANGED -- clamp(44px, 7.8vw, 128px), byte-for-byte,
       verified identical at all thirteen widths. R7 locked the value, the
       face, the four axes, the flat ground and the absent marks; only the
       place the number is written has moved.

       IT MOVED BECAUSE SOMETHING NOW DEPENDS ON IT. R15's Everyday Legends
       opener is declared as a fixed proportion of this size, and R8.2's rule
       is that a relationship which must hold is declared against its
       counterpart rather than left to fall out of two swept values. Left
       inline here, this clamp and that one would have been two independent
       floats agreeing by coincidence in one range -- the exact shape that
       shipped the Index numeral holding its ratio at 1440 and losing it at
       1024. */
    font-size: var(--quote-size);
    line-height: 1.02;
    letter-spacing: -0.028em;
    color: var(--porcelain);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  /* GOLD, 3 of 3 — the last of CONTEXT.md's pinned saturated type
     accents at rest, and the only colour in this section other than
     the Porcelain it sits in.

     Measured on the rendered pair: Antique Gold #AF8C5C on Obsidian
     #1A1613 is 5.76:1 and Porcelain #E7E2DD on Obsidian is 13.97:1.
     Both clear AA for normal text, let alone at 44-128px, at every
     band. Gold on Porcelain is 2.4:1 and on Cashmere 1.9:1 — under
     even the 3:1 large-text floor — so the dark ground is what lets
     this be the page's loudest gold moment at all.

     Only the colour changes. Same face, same axes, same size, same
     slope as the words around it, because the word is the end of the
     sentence and not a link. */
  .quote-line .legacy{
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--gold);
  }

  /* ---- COLLAPSE, stated before implementing ----------------------
     The column window's four bands are written out above `.win-quote`
     in global.css. What the BAND itself does:

     >= 1280    Full-bleed Obsidian. Type at columns 2-10, 112-128px,
                three lines at 1440 and 1920.
     1024-1279  Identical in kind. Same window, same padding, type
                scaling with the viewport. The folio hides at 1280 for
                its own reason and this section is the one the folio
                is already quiet over, so the boundary is invisible
                here.
     751-1023   8 tracks. Type at columns 2-7, one track in from the
                left trim and one of air at the right. Padding takes
                the clamp below.
     <= 750     One column, full measure, no inset — there is one
                track to be inset from. Padding at its floor.

     PADDING IN BANDS 3 AND 4. `clamp(116px, 14.67vw, 150px)`:
     150.07px at 1023, so the clamp's ceiling holds the 1024/1023
     boundary seamless to the flat value above it, and the taper
     starts at 1022. It reaches the 116px floor at 791 and holds it
     from there down through the whole phone band.

     116 IS NOT A ROUND NUMBER, IT IS A CONSTRAINT. Every other
     section on the page sets a FLAT pixel padding with no band
     override anywhere — Educational Leader and Athletic Management
     are 110 at every width from 1920 to 360. This section must stay
     the largest on the page at EVERY width, so its floor cannot go
     below 110; 116 keeps six pixels of headroom over the two tallest
     sections while still scaling the band down by a fifth on a phone.
     If a future pass gives any section a padding above 116, this
     floor moves with it. */
  @media (max-width: 1023px){
    .quote{ padding-block: clamp(116px, 14.67vw, 150px); }
  }
</style>
